<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Augusto César Sandino</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1895">1895</a>–<a href="/years/1934">1934</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Augusto César Sandino was a Nicaraguan revolutionary who led a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Marines occupying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> from 1927 to 1933, after rejecting the American-brokered peace that ended the Constitutionalist War. Operating from the northern mountains of Las <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segovia">Segovias</a>, he became a symbol of anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a> across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>. After the Marines left he made peace, but he was murdered in February 1934 on the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anastasio-somoza-garcia">Anastasio Somoza García</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/augusto-cesar-sandino.jpg" alt="Augusto César Sandino" width="320" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Augusto_C%C3%A9sar_Sandino.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sandino was born on 18 May 1895 at Niquinohomo, in the Masaya Department of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a>, the illegitimate son of Gregorio Sandino, a wealthy landowner, and Margarita Calderón, an indigenous servant of the family. At nine he went to live in his father's house and took his surname. In 1921 he shot and failed to kill a man who had insulted his mother, and fled, eventually to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, where he worked at a Standard Oil refinery near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tampico">Tampico</a> and took in anti-imperialist, anarchist and spiritist ideas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When Sandino returned, the Constitutionalist War had begun, in which Liberals under General José María Moncada rose against the Conservative president Adolfo Díaz. Sandino raised an army of gold miners and peasants. In May 1927 the Espino Negro accord, negotiated by the US envoy Henry L. Stimson, ended the fighting: both sides were to disarm and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-guard-dominican-republic">National Guard</a> would be formed. Sandino refused to disarm, returned to the mountains of Las Segovias, and in July declared war on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><p>On 16 July 1927 his men attacked US Marines at Ocotal and were repulsed with the help of one of the first dive-bombing attacks in history; the Marine commander estimated 300 of Sandino's men dead, though the actual number was about 80. The Marines never captured him, and in November 1927 they found his headquarters at El Chipote abandoned and guarded by straw dummies. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a> made overseas expeditions too costly, and the last Marines left in January 1933, after 130 of them had died. Sandino then met President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-bautista-sacasa">Juan Bautista Sacasa</a> and agreed to have his forces give up their weapons, but remained opposed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-guard-dominican-republic">National Guard</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 21 February 1934, after talks with Sacasa, Sandino, his brother Sócrates and two generals were stopped at the gate of the presidential palace by National Guardsmen, taken to a crossroads and executed. General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anastasio-somoza-garcia">Anastasio Somoza García</a> had ordered the killing without consulting Sacasa. Sandino's body has never been found. The Guard destroyed his army over the following month, and Somoza forced Sacasa to resign two years later and founded a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> for four decades.</p><p>Sandino became a symbol of resistance to American imperialism across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sandinista-national-liberation-front">Sandinista National Liberation Front</a>, founded in 1961, took his name and image and overthrew Somoza's son in 1979. In 2010 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a>'s congress named him a national hero.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Augusto César Sandino” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After a new civil war the Marines returned in 1926, and the liberal general <b>Augusto César Sandino</b> rejected the American-brokered peace of 1927, fighting a guerrilla war in the northern mountains until the Marines left in January 1933.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-nicaragua">Republic of Nicaragua · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Niquinohomo, in what is now <a href="/countries/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/managua">Managua</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=11.9,-86.1&amp;year=1920" aria-label="Niquinohomo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2183 1720 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Augusto César Sandino"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2633 1990h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2633 1990h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=11.9,-86.1&amp;year=1920">What was here in 1920? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
